<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Skeletal (fill-in) version of 4.4 The Chain Rule.

  Definitions, theorems, summaries, and problem statements are kept;
  derivations and solutions are replaced by blank workspace boxes, each
  with a one-line statement of what has to be done.  Content mirrors
  ch-14-4.ptx of the full lecture notes; every xml:id here is prefixed
  with "skel-" so the two can coexist.

  Figures: the first three tree diagrams and the saddle surface are
  reference pictures, so they are reproduced in full, as in the lecture
  notes.  Drawing a tree for a given composition is one of this section's
  objectives, so the last tree becomes a blank box for the reader to draw
  their own in.
-->
<section xml:id="sec-skel-fmv-chain-rule" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>The Chain Rule</title>

<objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
After completing this section, you should be able to:
</p>
</introduction>

<ul>
<li>
<p>
State and apply the chain rule for a function <m>w = f(x,y)</m> whose
intermediate variables <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are functions of a single
independent variable <m>t</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Extend the chain rule to three intermediate variables, and to two
independent variables <m>r</m> and <m>s</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Draw a tree diagram for a composition and read the corresponding chain
rule formula off the diagram.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Use the chain rule in applied settings, for instance to find the rate of
change of temperature experienced by an object moving along a path.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</objectives>

<introduction>
<p>
The chain rule for functions of one variable tells us how to
differentiate a composition. When a function of several variables has
inputs that themselves depend on other variables, there is a
corresponding rule, and it has one term for each intermediate variable.
Tree diagrams give a reliable way of writing these formulas down without
memorizing them.
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-cr-one-independent">
<title>One Independent Variable</title>

<theorem xml:id="thm-skel-chain-rule-two-intermediate">
<title>Chain Rule for Functions of Two Independent Variables</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the two-variable function <m>w = f(x,y)</m>. If the following
conditions are satisfied:
</p>
<p>
<alert>1.</alert> <m>w = f(x,y)</m> has continuous partial derivatives
<m>f_x</m> and <m>f_y</m>,
</p>
<p>
<alert>2.</alert> <m>x = x(t)</m> and <m>y = y(t)</m> are differentiable
functions of <m>t</m>,
</p>
<p>
then the function <m>w = f(x(t), y(t))</m> is a differentiable function
of <m>t</m> and
<md xml:id="eq-skel-chain-rule-two" number="yes">
\frac{dw}{dt}
= \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{dx}{dt}
+ \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{dy}{dt}.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-cr-tree-two">
<caption>
Tree diagram for <m>w = f(x(t), y(t))</m>. There are two paths from
<m>w</m> down to <m>t</m>, one through <m>x</m> and one through <m>y</m>,
and the chain rule adds one term for each.
</caption>
<image width="46%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="skel-prefigure-cr-tree-two">
<diagram dimensions="(280,300)" margins="14">
<coordinates bbox="(-1.7,-0.5,1.7,3.5)">
<line at="t2-wx" endpoints="((0,3),(-1.2,1.5))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="t2-wy" endpoints="((0,3),(1.2,1.5))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="t2-xt" endpoints="((-1.2,1.5),(0,0))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="t2-yt" endpoints="((1.2,1.5),(0,0))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<point at="t2-w" p="(0,3)" fill="black"/>
<point at="t2-x" p="(-1.2,1.5)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="t2-y" p="(1.2,1.5)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="t2-t" p="(0,0)" fill="#009E73"/>
<label at="t2-w-lab" anchor="(0,3.1)" alignment="n">
<m>w=f(x,y)</m>
</label>
<label at="t2-x-lab" anchor="(-1.3,1.5)" alignment="w" color="#D55E00">
<m>x</m>
</label>
<label at="t2-y-lab" anchor="(1.3,1.5)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>y</m>
</label>
<label at="t2-t-lab" anchor="(0,-0.12)" alignment="s" color="#009E73">
<m>t</m>
</label>
<label at="t2-wx-lab" anchor="(-0.72,2.4)" alignment="w" color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}</m>
</label>
<label at="t2-wy-lab" anchor="(0.72,2.4)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}</m>
</label>
<label at="t2-xt-lab" anchor="(-0.72,0.6)" alignment="w" color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{dx}{dt}</m>
</label>
<label at="t2-yt-lab" anchor="(0.72,0.6)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{dy}{dt}</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A diamond shaped tree diagram. At the top is w equals f of x y. Two dashed blue branches descend from it, the left labelled partial f partial x leading to a node x, and the right labelled partial f partial y leading to a node y. From x and y two further branches, labelled d x d t and d y d t, descend and meet at the bottom node t.">
  <annotation ref="t2-w" text="The dependent variable w at the top of the tree."/>
  <annotation ref="t2-x" text="The intermediate variable x."/>
  <annotation ref="t2-y" text="The intermediate variable y."/>
  <annotation ref="t2-t" text="The independent variable t at the bottom."/>
  <annotation ref="t2-wx" text="The branch from w to x contributes the factor partial f partial x."/>
  <annotation ref="t2-xt" text="The branch from x to t contributes the factor d x d t. Multiplying along this path gives the first term of the chain rule."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<xref ref="fig-skel-cr-tree-two"/> is how to remember that formula
without memorizing it. Work out the reading rule, and label the parts.
(Which variable is <term>dependent</term>, which are
<term>intermediate</term>, and which is <term>independent</term>? Then
describe how to get from the picture to
<xref ref="eq-skel-chain-rule-two"/>: something happens along each path,
and something else happens between paths. Count the paths and compare
with the number of terms.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,9);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6) -- (15,6);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.7) {Dependent: \qquad Intermediate: \qquad Independent:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.7) {Along each path we \dots, and between paths we \dots};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {Number of paths $=$ number of terms $=$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for naming the three kinds of variable, for the rule that turns the tree into the formula, and for the count of paths.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<theorem xml:id="thm-skel-chain-rule-three-intermediate">
<title>Chain Rule for Functions of Three Independent Variables</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the three-variable function <m>w = f(x,y,z)</m>. If the
following conditions are satisfied:
</p>
<p>
<alert>1.</alert> <m>w = f(x,y,z)</m> has continuous partial derivatives
<m>f_x</m>, <m>f_y</m>, and <m>f_z</m>,
</p>
<p>
<alert>2.</alert> <m>x = x(t)</m>, <m>y = y(t)</m>, and <m>z = z(t)</m>
are differentiable functions of <m>t</m>,
</p>
<p>
then the function <m>w = f(x(t), y(t), z(t))</m> is a differentiable
function of <m>t</m> and
<md xml:id="eq-skel-chain-rule-three" number="yes">
\frac{dw}{dt}
= \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{dx}{dt}
+ \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{dy}{dt}
+ \frac{\partial f}{\partial z}\frac{dz}{dt}.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-cr-tree-three">
<caption>
Tree diagram for <m>w = f(x(t), y(t), z(t))</m>. Each of the three
intermediate variables contributes one term to
<xref ref="eq-skel-chain-rule-three"/>.
</caption>
<image width="52%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="skel-prefigure-cr-tree-three">
<diagram dimensions="(320,300)" margins="14">
<coordinates bbox="(-2.1,-0.5,2.1,3.5)">
<line at="t3-wx" endpoints="((0,3),(-1.5,1.5))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="t3-wy" endpoints="((0,3),(0,1.5))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="t3-wz" endpoints="((0,3),(1.5,1.5))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="t3-xt" endpoints="((-1.5,1.5),(0,0))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="t3-yt" endpoints="((0,1.5),(0,0))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="t3-zt" endpoints="((1.5,1.5),(0,0))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<point at="t3-w" p="(0,3)" fill="black"/>
<point at="t3-x" p="(-1.5,1.5)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="t3-y" p="(0,1.5)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="t3-z" p="(1.5,1.5)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="t3-t" p="(0,0)" fill="#009E73"/>
<label at="t3-w-lab" anchor="(0,3.1)" alignment="n">
<m>w=f(x,y,z)</m>
</label>
<label at="t3-x-lab" anchor="(-1.6,1.5)" alignment="w" color="#D55E00">
<m>x</m>
</label>
<label at="t3-y-lab" anchor="(0.12,1.58)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>y</m>
</label>
<label at="t3-z-lab" anchor="(1.6,1.5)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>z</m>
</label>
<label at="t3-t-lab" anchor="(0,-0.12)" alignment="s" color="#009E73">
<m>t</m>
</label>
<label at="t3-wx-lab" anchor="(-0.95,2.4)" alignment="w" color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}</m>
</label>
<label at="t3-wy-lab" anchor="(-0.08,2.35)" alignment="w" color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}</m>
</label>
<label at="t3-wz-lab" anchor="(0.95,2.4)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial z}</m>
</label>
<label at="t3-xt-lab" anchor="(-0.95,0.6)" alignment="w" color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{dx}{dt}</m>
</label>
<label at="t3-yt-lab" anchor="(0.1,0.72)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{dy}{dt}</m>
</label>
<label at="t3-zt-lab" anchor="(0.95,0.6)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{dz}{dt}</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A tree diagram with w equals f of x y z at the top, three intermediate nodes x, y and z in a row below it, and the single node t at the bottom. Dashed blue branches connect w to each of the three intermediate variables and each of those down to t, giving three complete paths.">
  <annotation ref="t3-w" text="The dependent variable w at the top."/>
  <annotation ref="t3-y" text="The middle intermediate variable y, on the vertical branch."/>
  <annotation ref="t3-t" text="The independent variable t at the bottom. Because three separate paths lead from w to t, the chain rule has three terms."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-cr-xy-circle">
<title>Differentiating Along a Circle</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>w = f(x,y) = xy</m>, where <m>x = \cos t</m> and
<m>y = \sin t</m>. Evaluate
<m>\left.\dfrac{dw}{dt}\right|_{t=\pi/2}</m>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Do it twice. First with <xref ref="eq-skel-chain-rule-two"/>: the two
partials of <m>xy</m> are easy, and a double-angle identity tidies the
result. Then again by substituting <m>x(t)</m> and <m>y(t)</m> into
<m>f</m> first and differentiating the one-variable function that
results. The two answers must agree; say what the second method shows
about when the chain rule is worth using at all.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,15);
\draw[gray!35] (0,9.5) -- (15,9.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4) -- (15,4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,14.7) {Method 1, with the chain rule:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,9.2) {Method 2, substituting first:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.7) {When is the chain rule worth it?};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the chain rule computation, for the same derivative found by substituting first, and for a comment on when each method is preferable.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
Geometrically, the constraint <m>x = \cos t</m>, <m>y = \sin t</m>
confines the point <m>(x,y)</m> to the unit circle
<m>x^2+y^2 = 1</m>, so the composition traces a curve on the saddle
<m>z = xy</m>, as shown in <xref ref="fig-skel-cr-saddle"/>. The
derivative <m>\frac{dw}{dt}</m> is the rate at which the height of that
curve changes; check the sign of your answer against the picture at
<m>t = \pi/2</m>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-cr-saddle">
<caption>
The curve traced on the surface <m>z = xy</m> as <m>(x,y)</m> runs around
the unit circle <m>x^2+y^2=1</m>. Marked are the points at <m>t = 0</m>,
<m>t = \pi/4</m>, and <m>t = \pi/2</m>.
</caption>
<image width="100%">
<description>
<p>
A saddle shaped surface, the graph of z equals x y, shaded blue where it
dips below the x y plane and gold where it rises above it. A closed green
curve is drawn on the surface, lying above the unit circle in the x y
plane, which is drawn in dark purple. Three points on the curve are marked
in magenta, red and black, corresponding to t equals zero, t equals pi
over four, and t equals pi over two. A legend above the axes names the
surface, the circle, the curve and the three marked points.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\fontfamily{phv}\fontsize{10}{12.5}\selectfont]
\begin{axis}[
  width=12cm, height=8.5cm,
  view={-35}{24},
  xmin=-1.45, xmax=1.45, ymin=-1.45, ymax=1.45, zmin=-0.95, zmax=0.95,
  xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
  xtick={-1,0,1}, ytick={-1,0,1}, ztick={-0.5,0,0.5},
  clip=false,
  % blue where the surface falls below the xy-plane, gold where it rises above
  % it, so the two rising and two falling quarters of the trip read apart
  colormap={saddle}{rgb255(0cm)=(70,148,190)
                    rgb255(1cm)=(238,240,242)
                    rgb255(2cm)=(228,158,72)},
  point meta min=-0.9, point meta max=0.9,
  legend cell align=left,
  legend columns=2,
  legend style={
    at={(0.5,1.03)}, anchor=south,
    draw=gray!50, fill=white, fill opacity=0.92, text opacity=1,
    inner sep=5pt, row sep=2pt,
    /tikz/every even column/.append style={column sep=10pt},
    font=\fontfamily{phv}\fontsize{10}{12.5}\selectfont,
  },
]
% the saddle z = xy, drawn over the disc that the unit circle sits inside so
% that it stays within the axis box instead of running off the top and bottom
% shader=flat, not interp: an interpolated shader emits a PDF smooth shading,
% which the pdf-to-svg step flattens to a low-resolution bitmap and leaves the
% rim of the disc visibly pixelated. Flat cells stay vector, and at this mesh
% density they read as a continuous gradient anyway.
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat corner, point meta=z,
          fill opacity=0.8, draw=mapped color, draw opacity=0.8, line width=0.1pt,
          z buffer=sort, forget plot,
          domain=0:1.32, y domain=0:360, samples=17, samples y=97]
  ({x*cos(y)},{x*sin(y)},{x^2*cos(y)*sin(y)});
% the unit circle in the xy-plane
\addplot3[very thick, violet!75!black, forget plot, domain=0:360, samples=64]
  ({cos(x)},{sin(x)},{0});
% the curve on the surface: (cos t, sin t, cos t sin t)
\addplot3[ultra thick, green!45!black, forget plot, domain=0:360, samples=80]
  ({cos(x)},{sin(x)},{cos(x)*sin(x)});
% marked points, ringed in white so they read against either colour
\addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=2.2, color=magenta, forget plot,
          mark options={fill=magenta, draw=white, line width=0.5pt}]
  coordinates {(1,0,0)};
\addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=2.2, color=red!70!black, forget plot,
          mark options={fill=red!70!black, draw=white, line width=0.5pt}]
  coordinates {(0.7071,0.7071,0.5)};
\addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=2.2, color=black, forget plot,
          mark options={fill=black, draw=white, line width=0.5pt}]
  coordinates {(0,1,0)};
% the legend: the three objects on the left, the three marked points on the
% right, in the order they are met as t runs from 0 to pi/2
\addlegendimage{area legend, draw=black!25, shading=axis,
                left color=blue!30, right color=orange!45}
\addlegendentry{the surface $z=xy$}
\addlegendimage{only marks, mark=*, mark size=2.2, color=magenta,
                mark options={fill=magenta, draw=white, line width=0.5pt}}
\addlegendentry{$t=0:\;(1,0,0)$}
\addlegendimage{very thick, violet!75!black}
\addlegendentry{the circle $x^2+y^2=1$}
\addlegendimage{only marks, mark=*, mark size=2.2, color=red!70!black,
                mark options={fill=red!70!black, draw=white, line width=0.5pt}}
\addlegendentry{$t=\frac{\pi}{4}:\;\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt2},\frac{1}{\sqrt2},\frac12\right)$}
\addlegendimage{ultra thick, green!45!black}
\addlegendentry{the traced curve}
\addlegendimage{only marks, mark=*, mark size=2.2, color=black,
                mark options={fill=black, draw=white, line width=0.5pt}}
\addlegendentry{$t=\frac{\pi}{2}:\;(0,1,0)$}
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<xref ref="fig-skel-cr-saddle-interactive"/> sets that picture moving. Send the
point round the circle with the slider, or jump straight to
<m>t = 0</m>, <m>t = \pi/4</m> and <m>t = \pi/2</m>; the readout carries both
the height <m>w</m> and the rate <m>dw/dt</m>. Check the value you computed
against the reading at <m>t = \pi/2</m>, and note how many times the curve
climbs and falls in one trip round the circle <mdash/> your formula for
<m>dw/dt</m> should predict that.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-cr-saddle-interactive">
<caption>
<xref ref="fig-skel-cr-saddle"/>, set moving. Send the point round the circle
with the slider, or
jump to <m>t = 0</m>, <m>t = \pi/4</m> and <m>t = \pi/2</m>: the legend
gives the height <m>w = \tfrac12\sin 2t</m> of the curve and the rate
<m>\frac{dw}{dt} = \cos 2t</m> at which that height is changing, which is
the number the chain rule computes.
</caption>
<interactive xml:id="interactive-skel-cr-saddle"
             iframe="saddle-circle-manim.html" width="100%" aspect="3:2">
<instructions>
<p>
The saddle is <m>z = xy</m>, the mauve ring below it is the unit circle
<m>x = \cos t</m>, <m>y = \sin t</m>, and the green curve is the path
traced on the saddle as <m>t</m> increases. The white point marks the
current <m>t</m>; the arrow on it points along the curve and grows with
<m>\left|\frac{dw}{dt}\right|</m>, turning amber where the height is
rising and red where it is falling. The three coloured dots are the
marked points <m>t=0</m>, <m>t=\pi/4</m> and <m>t=\pi/2</m>. Drag the
picture to turn it.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

</statement>
</example>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-cr-second">
<title>A Second Application of the Chain Rule</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>w = f(x,y) = xy + y^2</m>, where
<m>x = \cos t</m> and <m>y = 1 + 2\sin t</m>. Evaluate
<m>\left.\dfrac{dw}{dt}\right|_{t=\pi}</m>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Same routine as before, but here substituting first would be messy, so
use <xref ref="eq-skel-chain-rule-two"/>. Compute the two partials and
the two ordinary derivatives separately before combining, and substitute
<m>t = \pi</m> only at the very end.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,13);
\draw[gray!35] (0,10) -- (15,10);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4) -- (15,4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,12.7) {$f_x =$ \quad $f_y =$ \quad $dx/dt =$ \quad $dy/dt =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,9.7) {$\dfrac{dw}{dt} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.7) {At $t = \pi$:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the four ingredient derivatives, for the assembled derivative, and for its value at t equals pi.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-cr-two-independent">
<title>Two Independent Variables</title>

<theorem xml:id="thm-skel-chain-rule-rs">
<title>Chain Rule for Two Independent and Two Intermediate Variables</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the two-variable function <m>w = f(x,y)</m>. If
<m>w = f(x,y)</m>, <m>x = x(r,s)</m>, and <m>y = y(r,s)</m> are
differentiable functions, then the partial derivatives of the function
<m>w(r,s) = f(x(r,s), y(r,s))</m> are given by
<md xml:id="eq-skel-chain-rule-rs" number="yes">
\frac{\partial w}{\partial r}
= \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{\partial x}{\partial r}
+ \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{\partial y}{\partial r}
\qquad \text{and} \qquad
\frac{\partial w}{\partial s}
= \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{\partial x}{\partial s}
+ \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{\partial y}{\partial s}.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-cr-tree-rs">
<caption>
Two readings of the same tree for two independent variables. On the left,
the branches leading to <m>r</m> give
<m>\frac{\partial w}{\partial r}</m>; on the right, the branches leading
to <m>s</m> give <m>\frac{\partial w}{\partial s}</m>.
</caption>
<image width="92%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="skel-prefigure-cr-tree-rs">
<diagram dimensions="(600,300)" margins="14">
<coordinates bbox="(-1.9,-0.9,7.9,3.6)">
<!-- left tree: highlight branches to r -->
<line at="rs-l-wx" endpoints="((0,3),(-1.2,1.6))" stroke="#D55E00"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2.5"/>
<line at="rs-l-wy" endpoints="((0,3),(1.2,1.6))" stroke="#D55E00"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2.5"/>
<line at="rs-l-xr" endpoints="((-1.2,1.6),(-0.8,0))" stroke="#D55E00"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2.5"/>
<line at="rs-l-yr" endpoints="((1.2,1.6),(-0.8,0))" stroke="#D55E00"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2.5"/>
<line at="rs-l-xs" endpoints="((-1.2,1.6),(0.8,0))" stroke="#CCCCCC"
      dash="3 3" thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="rs-l-ys" endpoints="((1.2,1.6),(0.8,0))" stroke="#CCCCCC"
      dash="3 3" thickness="1.5"/>
<point at="rs-l-w" p="(0,3)" fill="black"/>
<point at="rs-l-x" p="(-1.2,1.6)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="rs-l-y" p="(1.2,1.6)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="rs-l-r" p="(-0.8,0)" fill="#009E73"/>
<point at="rs-l-s" p="(0.8,0)" fill="#009E73"/>
<label at="rs-l-w-lab" anchor="(0,3.1)" alignment="n"><m>w=f(x,y)</m></label>
<label at="rs-l-x-lab" anchor="(-1.3,1.6)" alignment="w" color="#D55E00"><m>x</m></label>
<label at="rs-l-y-lab" anchor="(1.3,1.6)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00"><m>y</m></label>
<label at="rs-l-r-lab" anchor="(-0.8,-0.12)" alignment="s" color="#009E73"><m>r</m></label>
<label at="rs-l-s-lab" anchor="(0.8,-0.12)" alignment="s" color="#999999"><m>s</m></label>
<label at="rs-l-eq" anchor="(0,-0.62)" alignment="c" color="#D55E00">
<m>\frac{\partial w}{\partial r}=\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{\partial x}{\partial r}+\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{\partial y}{\partial r}</m>
</label>
<!-- right tree: highlight branches to s -->
<line at="rs-r-wx" endpoints="((6,3),(4.8,1.6))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2.5"/>
<line at="rs-r-wy" endpoints="((6,3),(7.2,1.6))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2.5"/>
<line at="rs-r-xs" endpoints="((4.8,1.6),(6.8,0))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2.5"/>
<line at="rs-r-ys" endpoints="((7.2,1.6),(6.8,0))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2.5"/>
<line at="rs-r-xr" endpoints="((4.8,1.6),(5.2,0))" stroke="#CCCCCC"
      dash="3 3" thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="rs-r-yr" endpoints="((7.2,1.6),(5.2,0))" stroke="#CCCCCC"
      dash="3 3" thickness="1.5"/>
<point at="rs-r-w" p="(6,3)" fill="black"/>
<point at="rs-r-x" p="(4.8,1.6)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="rs-r-y" p="(7.2,1.6)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="rs-r-r" p="(5.2,0)" fill="#009E73"/>
<point at="rs-r-s" p="(6.8,0)" fill="#009E73"/>
<label at="rs-r-w-lab" anchor="(6,3.1)" alignment="n"><m>w=f(x,y)</m></label>
<label at="rs-r-x-lab" anchor="(4.7,1.6)" alignment="w" color="#D55E00"><m>x</m></label>
<label at="rs-r-y-lab" anchor="(7.3,1.6)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00"><m>y</m></label>
<label at="rs-r-r-lab" anchor="(5.2,-0.12)" alignment="s" color="#999999"><m>r</m></label>
<label at="rs-r-s-lab" anchor="(6.8,-0.12)" alignment="s" color="#009E73"><m>s</m></label>
