<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Two copies of the same tree diagram side by side. Each has w equals f of x y at the top, the intermediate variables x and y below it, and the two independent variables r and s at the bottom. In the left copy the branches leading down to r are highlighted in orange and the branches to s are faint grey; in the right copy the branches leading to s are highlighted in blue instead. Under each tree is the corresponding chain rule formula.">
  <annotation ref="rs-l-xr" text="In the left tree, the highlighted branches are those running from w through x and through y down to r, giving the formula for the partial derivative of w with respect to r."/>
  <annotation ref="rs-r-xs" text="In the right tree, the highlighted branches run down to s instead, giving the formula for the partial derivative of w with respect to s."/>
  <annotation ref="rs-l-s" text="The faint grey branches are the ones not used for this particular derivative."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
Say what changed. (The tree now has two variables at the bottom, so it is
read twice. State which branches you follow for each reading, and why the
derivatives at the top of the tree became partial derivatives when they
were ordinary ones in <xref ref="eq-skel-chain-rule-two"/>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.7) {Reading the tree for $r$, then for $s$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {Why the lower derivatives are now partial:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, for how the tree is read twice and for why the derivatives are now partial derivatives.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-cr-exp-sin">
<title>Two Independent Variables</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>z = f(x,y) = e^x \sin y</m>, where
<m>x = st^2</m> and <m>y = s^2 t</m>, and evaluate
<m>\dfrac{\partial z}{\partial s}</m> and
<m>\dfrac{\partial z}{\partial t}</m>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Here <m>s</m> and <m>t</m> are the independent variables. Lay out all six
ingredient derivatives first <mdash/> two for <m>f</m>, and four for
<m>x</m> and <m>y</m> <mdash/> then assemble each answer with
<xref ref="eq-skel-chain-rule-rs"/>. Finish by writing everything back in
terms of <m>s</m> and <m>t</m>, so no <m>x</m> or <m>y</m> is left.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,15);
\draw[gray!35] (0,11) -- (15,11);
\draw[gray!35] (0,5.5) -- (15,5.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,14.7) {The six ingredient derivatives:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.7) {$\dfrac{\partial z}{\partial s} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.2) {$\dfrac{\partial z}{\partial t} =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the six ingredient derivatives and for each of the two partial derivatives of z.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-cr-applications">
<title>Applications</title>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-cr-ant">
<title>The Temperature Experienced by a Moving Ant</title>
<statement>
<p>
Suppose that the temperature on a metal plate is given by the function
<m>T</m> with
<md>
T(x,y) = 100 - (x^2 + 4y^2),
</md>
where the temperature is measured in degrees Fahrenheit and <m>x</m> and
<m>y</m> are each measured in feet. Now suppose that an ant is walking on
the metal plate in such a way that it walks in a straight line from the
point <m>(1,4)</m> to the point <m>(5,6)</m>.
<ol marker="a.">
<li>
<p>
Find parametric equations <m>(x(t), y(t))</m> for the ant's coordinates
as it walks the line from <m>(1,4)</m> to <m>(5,6)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
What can you say about <m>\frac{dx}{dt}</m> and <m>\frac{dy}{dt}</m> for
every value of <m>t</m>?
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Determine the instantaneous rate of change in temperature with respect to
<m>t</m> that the ant is experiencing at the moment it is halfway from
<m>(1,4)</m> to <m>(5,6)</m>, using your parametric equations for
<m>x</m> and <m>y</m>. Include units on your answer.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(For <alert>a</alert>, parametrize the segment as in
<xref ref="sec-skel-geom-parametrization"/>; state the range of <m>t</m>
and check both endpoints. For <alert>b</alert>, the answer is a property
of straight-line motion <mdash/> say what it is and why. For
<alert>c</alert>, the tree is <xref ref="fig-skel-cr-tree-two"/> with
<m>T</m> in place of <m>w</m>; find the halfway value of <m>t</m> first,
and do not forget the units, which come from
<m>{}^\circ\text{F}</m> and the unit of <m>t</m>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,17);
\draw[gray!35] (0,12.5) -- (15,12.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,9.5) -- (15,9.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4) -- (15,4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,16.7) {a. $x(t) =$ \qquad $y(t) =$ \qquad range of $t$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,12.2) {b.};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,9.2) {c. $\dfrac{dT}{dt} =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.7) {\phantom{c. }Halfway, $t =$ \qquad so the rate is \qquad\qquad units:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for the parametric equations with the range of the parameter, for the observation about the two derivatives, for the rate of change of temperature, and for its value halfway along with units.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-cr-identity">
<title>Proving an Identity with the Chain Rule</title>
<statement>
<p>
Suppose that <m>w = f(xz, yz)</m>, where <m>f</m> is a differentiable
function. Show that
<md>
x\frac{\partial w}{\partial x} + y\frac{\partial w}{\partial y}
= z\frac{\partial w}{\partial z}.
</md>
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Name the intermediate variables <m>u = xz</m> and <m>v = yz</m> first,
so that <m>w = f(u,v)</m>, and draw the tree in the box below. Notice that
<m>z</m> appears under <em>both</em> intermediate variables while <m>x</m>
and <m>y</m> appear under only one each <mdash/> that asymmetry is what
makes the identity work. Read the three partial derivatives off your tree,
then form each side of the identity and compare.)
</p>

<sidebyside widths="40% 56%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (6,7);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,6.7) {Tree diagram:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box for the reader to draw the tree diagram of the composition.</shortdescription>
</image>
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (8.4,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4.6) -- (8.4,4.6);
\draw[gray!35] (0,2.3) -- (8.4,2.3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,6.7) {$w_x =$ \qquad $w_y =$ \qquad $w_z =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,4.3) {Left side:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,2.0) {Right side:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the three partial derivatives read off the tree and for each side of the identity.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

</subsection>

</section>
